/li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/vajiralongkorn"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/vajiralongkorn.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Vajiralongkorn</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Vajiralongkorn, also known as Rama X, has been king of Thailand since 2016, succeeding his father Bhumibol Adulyadej.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/vajiravudh"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/vajiravudh.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Vajiravudh</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Vajiravudh, Rama VI, was king of Siam from 1910 to 1925, educated at Sandhurst and Oxford.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/vajrayana"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/vajrayana.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Vajrayana</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">Vajrayana, or Tantric Buddhism, is a form of Mahayana Buddhism that developed in India from around the 7th century, using ritual, mantras, mandalas and initiation by a…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/vakhsh-river"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/vakhsh-river.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Vakhsh River</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Vakhsh is a river of Tajikistan, about 520 km long, formed by the Kyzylsu, which rises in Kyrgyzstan, and flowing south-west to join the Panj and form the Amu Darya.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/vakhtang-i-of-iberia"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/vakhtang-i-of-iberia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Vakhtang I of Iberia</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Vakhtang I Gorgasali was king of Iberia, in eastern Georgia, in the second half of the 5th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/vakhtang-vi-of-kartli"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/vakhtang-vi-of-kartli.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Vakhtang VI of Kartli</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Vakhtang VI was king of Kartli in eastern Georgia, ruling as regent from 1703 and as king in 1716 and from 1719 to 1724 under Safavid overlordship.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/vakil"><span class="enc-name">Vakil</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">Vakil was a Persian title meaning deputy or regent, used for a high office of state in Safavid Iran.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/vakil-al-ruaya"><span class="enc-name">Vakil al-Ruaya</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">Vakil al-Ruaya, meaning Regent of the People, was the title adopted by Karim Khan, founder of the Zand Empire.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/vakil-bath"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/vakil-bath.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Vakil Bath</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Vakil Bath is a historic public bathhouse in Shiraz, Iran, built under Karim Khan during the Zand period.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/vakil-bazaar"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/vakil-bazaar.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Vakil Bazaar</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Vakil Bazaar is the main covered market of Shiraz, Iran, built under Karim Khan during the Zand period.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/vakil-mosque"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/vakil-mosque.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Vakil Mosque</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Vakil Mosque is a mosque in Shiraz, Iran, built under Karim Khan during the Zand period, next to the Vakil Bazaar.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/vakpati-munja"><span class="enc-name">Vakpati Munja</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Vakpati Munja was a king of the Paramara dynasty of Malwa in central India in the late 10th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/vakpatiraja-poet"><span class="enc-name">Vakpatiraja (poet)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Vakpatiraja was a poet of the court of King Yashovarman of Kannauj in the 8th century, writing in Maharashtri Prakrit.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/val-daran"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/val-daran.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Val d'Aran</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Val d'Aran is a valley in the central Pyrenees, in Catalonia, Spain, lying on the northern side of the main ridge and drained by the Garonne.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/val-dossola"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/val-dossola.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Val d'Ossola</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Val d'Ossola is an Alpine valley system in the north of Piedmont, Italy, drained by the river Toce and bordered on three sides by Switzerland.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/val-di-chiana"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/val-di-chiana.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Val di Chiana</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Val di Chiana is a broad valley in eastern Tuscany and western Umbria, running from near Arezzo south to Chiusi and Lake Trasimeno.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/valabhi"><span class="enc-name">Valabhi</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Valabhi was an ancient city on the Saurashtra peninsula of Gujarat, near modern Vallabhipur, and the capital of the Maitraka dynasty from the late 5th to the 8th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/valais"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/valais.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Valais</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Valais is a canton in south-western Switzerland covering the upper valley of the Rhône, with Sion as its capital.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/valdas-adamkus"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/valdas-adamkus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Valdas Adamkus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Valdas Adamkus is a Lithuanian-American environmental official and politician who was president of Lithuania from 1998 to 2003 and from 2004 to 2009.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/valdemar-i-of-denmark"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/valdemar-i-of-denmark.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Valdemar I of Denmark</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Valdemar I, called the Great, was King of Denmark from 1157 to 1182, ending a long period of civil war.</span></a></li><l