ss="enc-name">Van Fortress</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Van Fortress, or Van Kalesi, is a massive citadel on a long limestone ridge on the eastern shore of Lake Van in eastern Turkey.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/vanand"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/vanand.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Vanand</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Vanand was a historical district of Armenia in the province of Ayrarat, centred on the fortress town of Kars in present-day north-eastern Turkey.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/vanchi"><span class="enc-name">Vanchi</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Vanchi was the capital of the early Chera kings according to Sangam literature.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/vandad-hormozd"><span class="enc-name">Vandad Hormozd</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Vandad Hormozd was a ruler of the Qarinvand dynasty in the Alborz mountains of Tabaristan, northern Iran, in the late 8th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/vandal-conquest-of-north-africa"><span class="enc-name">Vandal conquest of North Africa</span><span class="enc-type">Campaign · 429–439 CE</span><span class="enc-snip">The Vandal conquest of North Africa was the seizure of Roman Africa by the Vandals and Alans between 429 and 439 CE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/vandalic-war"><span class="enc-name">Vandalic War</span><span class="enc-type">War · 533–534</span><span class="enc-snip">The Vandalic War was fought in 533–534 between the Byzantine Empire of Justinian I and the Vandal Kingdom in North Africa.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/vandals"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/vandals.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Vandals</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Vandals were a Germanic people who migrated through Gaul and Spain before crossing into North Africa in 429 CE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/vanga"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/vanga.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Vanga</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Vanga was an ancient kingdom and region of the Ganges delta in south-eastern Bengal, roughly central and southern Bangladesh.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/vangio"><span class="enc-name">Vangio</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Vangio was a Suebian king of the 1st century CE, nephew of Vannius, the client king installed by Rome north of the Danube.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/vani"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/vani.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Vani</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Vani is a town in western Georgia and the site of an important ancient settlement of Colchis, on a hill above the Sulori river.</span></a></li></ul>
